1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:08,710
SecuRAG answers questions about your own security
documents. Everything runs on one machine — nothing leaves
the network.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:18,341
Settings shows what it is made of: Postgres, ChromaDB and
Ollama, all local, and the retrieval parameters in the
open.

3
00:00:18,441 --> 00:00:23,442
A new document goes in — the NIST Cybersecurity Framework.

4
00:00:24,441 --> 00:00:30,129
It is chunked and embedded on upload, and the table says
when it is ready to answer.

5
00:00:31,941 --> 00:00:36,841
The knowledge base goes from 31 chunks to 129.

6
00:00:43,441 --> 00:00:51,804
A question runs through three visible stages, each with
its own timer: the input is checked, the store is
searched, the model writes.

7
00:00:51,941 --> 00:00:56,156
The answer cites the document and the page it came from.

8
00:00:56,941 --> 00:01:02,351
Opening a citation shows the same passage in the original
PDF.

9
00:01:05,441 --> 00:01:13,924
A follow-up keeps the thread: retrieval runs again over
the same knowledge base, and the answer builds on the
first rather than starting over.

10
00:01:22,941 --> 00:01:31,708
A third question, on incident response, comes back with
the specific subcategories the framework defines — each
one cited to its page.

11
00:01:40,441 --> 00:01:46,791
A prompt injection attempt. It clears the input guardrail,
and the model refuses it anyway.
